WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.453 --> 00:00:08.170
- Season eight of HBO's "Game of Thrones"

00:00:08.170 --> 00:00:11.390
has come to an end, and a lot
of people, including myself,

00:00:11.390 --> 00:00:13.790
are pretty annoyed
about how it played out.

00:00:13.790 --> 00:00:16.610
In this video, I'm going to
break down the main reasons

00:00:16.610 --> 00:00:18.720
why fans were left so disappointed

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:21.046
by what was supposed to
be a fitting conclusion

00:00:21.046 --> 00:00:24.093
to one of the greatest
TV shows of all time.

00:00:25.480 --> 00:00:28.090
No. 1, it was rushed.

00:00:28.090 --> 00:00:29.950
Everything in season eight
seemed to be happening

00:00:29.950 --> 00:00:31.970
too quickly, and it would often feel like

00:00:31.970 --> 00:00:33.820
three episodes worth of information

00:00:33.820 --> 00:00:36.898
was being crammed into one
feature-length episode.

00:00:36.898 --> 00:00:39.450
A side effect of this
was that we weren't able

00:00:39.450 --> 00:00:41.130
to enjoy the big payoff moments

00:00:41.130 --> 00:00:43.210
in the way that we had previously.

00:00:43.210 --> 00:00:45.810
For example, in episode
one of season eight,

00:00:45.810 --> 00:00:47.340
Jon rode Rhaegal.

00:00:47.340 --> 00:00:50.030
This is something that
has huge significance,

00:00:50.030 --> 00:00:51.980
but it didn't feel like a big event,

00:00:51.980 --> 00:00:54.220
and the reason is it came
halfway through an episode

00:00:54.220 --> 00:00:56.940
where loads of other really
important things happened.

00:00:56.940 --> 00:00:59.370
Jon was reunited with Bran and Arya,

00:00:59.370 --> 00:01:02.978
Theon saved Yara, the Golden
Company arrived in Westeros,

00:01:02.978 --> 00:01:05.530
Ned Umber was killed by the White Walkers,

00:01:05.530 --> 00:01:08.440
Jaime Lannister turned up
unannounced at Winterfell

00:01:08.440 --> 00:01:10.100
and had a stare-off with Bran,

00:01:10.100 --> 00:01:12.818
and Jon actually learned
that he was Aegon Targaryen,

00:01:12.818 --> 00:01:16.180
heir to the Iron Throne and
nephew of his lover, Dany.

00:01:16.180 --> 00:01:18.790
Yeah, a lot of stuff
happened in that episode.

00:01:18.790 --> 00:01:20.340
When you compare this moment to when

00:01:20.340 --> 00:01:22.340
Daenerys first flew with Drogon,

00:01:22.340 --> 00:01:24.540
it pales in insignificance.

00:01:24.540 --> 00:01:27.540
It was the climax of
season five, episode nine,

00:01:27.540 --> 00:01:30.720
and was part of a tense
nine-and-a-half-minute scene

00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:33.540
where Daenerys was ambushed
by the Sons of the Harpy

00:01:33.540 --> 00:01:35.474
in the fighting pits of Meereen.

00:01:35.474 --> 00:01:37.760
Drogon, who was previously missing,

00:01:37.760 --> 00:01:39.800
swooped down to protect Dany

00:01:39.800 --> 00:01:42.896
before she climbed on
its back and flew away.

00:01:42.896 --> 00:01:45.960
The early seasons of "Game of
Thrones" were dialogue-heavy,

00:01:45.960 --> 00:01:47.010
building up characters,

00:01:47.010 --> 00:01:49.340
and there wasn't much on-screen action.

00:01:49.340 --> 00:01:51.890
Season eight, and to some
degree six and seven,

00:01:51.890 --> 00:01:53.680
were not like this at all.

00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:56.750
No. 2, character development.

00:01:56.750 --> 00:01:59.030
Something that seemed
like a direct consequence

00:01:59.030 --> 00:02:01.225
of the rushed nature of season eight

00:02:01.225 --> 00:02:04.250
was the jarring character
development, which left viewers

00:02:04.250 --> 00:02:07.327
feeling shocked, confused,
and even betrayed.

00:02:07.327 --> 00:02:10.020
Many would argue that
the show lost its way

00:02:10.020 --> 00:02:12.030
from season five onwards.

00:02:12.030 --> 00:02:14.120
That was the point that Benioff and Weiss

00:02:14.120 --> 00:02:16.120
started making the biggest departures

00:02:16.120 --> 00:02:18.630
from George R.R. Martin's books,

00:02:18.630 --> 00:02:22.130
but season eight has had
by far the most backlash.

00:02:22.130 --> 00:02:23.035
Here's why.

00:02:23.035 --> 00:02:26.320
Each and every character
had been painted so vividly

00:02:26.320 --> 00:02:28.981
up until this point,
and within six episodes,

00:02:28.981 --> 00:02:31.560
some had changed beyond recognition,

00:02:31.560 --> 00:02:34.500
and the best example of
this is Daenerys Targaryen.

00:02:34.500 --> 00:02:36.730
We had seven seasons of Daenerys Targaryen

00:02:36.730 --> 00:02:39.630
being built up as one of
the main heroes in the show,

00:02:39.630 --> 00:02:41.350
and she was torn down and destroyed

00:02:41.350 --> 00:02:43.270
in the final three episodes.

00:02:43.270 --> 00:02:45.730
Many fans of the show felt betrayed by it.

00:02:45.730 --> 00:02:47.560
I just want to make it
clear that this isn't me

00:02:47.560 --> 00:02:49.410
criticizing the story arc.

00:02:49.410 --> 00:02:51.817
If it was necessary for Dany
to become the Mad Queen,

00:02:51.817 --> 00:02:53.510
then that is fine,

00:02:53.510 --> 00:02:55.861
but it should not have
happened so quickly.

00:02:55.861 --> 00:02:58.290
I think I could have gone
along with her downfall

00:02:58.290 --> 00:03:00.590
if I was subjected to
at least three episodes

00:03:00.590 --> 00:03:03.190
of her decline following
the death of Missandei.

00:03:03.190 --> 00:03:05.890
I feel like we needed
to see her not eating,

00:03:05.890 --> 00:03:07.770
not sleeping, and growing more and more

00:03:07.770 --> 00:03:09.330
paranoid and vengeful.

00:03:09.330 --> 00:03:11.930
Instead what we got was a
few shots of her looking

00:03:11.930 --> 00:03:15.560
a bit tired with bags under her
eyes and her hair messed up.

00:03:15.560 --> 00:03:17.770
This just wasn't good enough.

00:03:17.770 --> 00:03:19.870
When the bells rang out
around King's Landing

00:03:19.870 --> 00:03:22.470
and Dany decided to burn
down the city anyway,

00:03:22.470 --> 00:03:24.800
killing thousands of
people in the process,

00:03:24.800 --> 00:03:26.400
it just didn't feel like a decision

00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:29.570
that the Daenerys we'd followed
for eight season would make.

00:03:29.570 --> 00:03:31.490
OK, let's move on to Jon.

00:03:31.490 --> 00:03:34.070
Jon had the most screen time
throughout the whole show

00:03:34.070 --> 00:03:36.490
and probably the most
interesting character arc

00:03:36.490 --> 00:03:37.720
out of anyone else,

00:03:37.720 --> 00:03:40.950
the secret son of Rhaegar
Targaryen and Lyanna Stark,

00:03:40.950 --> 00:03:44.425
the man who is an actual
mixture of ice and fire.

00:03:44.425 --> 00:03:47.167
What was the point of
Jon being a Targaryen?

00:03:47.167 --> 00:03:50.030
The big reveal of this
information was a huge part

00:03:50.030 --> 00:03:52.740
of the narrative, yet it
was only used as a tool

00:03:52.740 --> 00:03:56.710
to partly justify the descent
of Daenerys into madness.

00:03:56.710 --> 00:03:58.500
Finally on character development,

00:03:58.500 --> 00:04:01.160
let's look at Jaime
Lannister, the Kingslayer.

00:04:01.160 --> 00:04:03.340
Season one's most-loathed character

00:04:03.340 --> 00:04:05.340
was transformed throughout eight seasons

00:04:05.340 --> 00:04:07.280
as we learned more and more about his past

00:04:07.280 --> 00:04:10.680
and saw him perform
noble and selfless acts.

00:04:10.680 --> 00:04:12.530
This all peaked when he deserted Cersei

00:04:12.530 --> 00:04:15.320
to go and fight at
Winterfell to save Westeros

00:04:15.320 --> 00:04:17.480
from the Night King and
the Army of the Dead.

00:04:17.480 --> 00:04:21.050
After this, he again deserts
a lover, Brienne this time,

00:04:21.050 --> 00:04:23.660
to go and save Cersei,
but this move isn't quite

00:04:23.660 --> 00:04:25.750
the selfless act that the previous one was

00:04:25.750 --> 00:04:28.130
and actually reduces
a lot of the hard work

00:04:28.130 --> 00:04:30.190
that was put into building him up.

00:04:30.190 --> 00:04:31.860
Were we supposed to think
that this commitment

00:04:31.860 --> 00:04:33.322
to his sibling was noble?

00:04:33.322 --> 00:04:35.540
It certainly didn't seem it.

00:04:35.540 --> 00:04:38.480
Three, anticlimactic moments.

00:04:38.480 --> 00:04:41.750
By far, the biggest
anticlimactic moment for me

00:04:41.750 --> 00:04:44.390
in season eight was the
death of the Night King.

00:04:44.390 --> 00:04:48.040
Yes, the battle was huge
and cinematically beautiful,

00:04:48.040 --> 00:04:49.630
when you brightened it up a little bit,

00:04:49.630 --> 00:04:51.570
but it wasn't what some
"Game of Thrones" fans

00:04:51.570 --> 00:04:53.870
were waiting for. Here's why.

00:04:53.870 --> 00:04:56.690
The opening scenes of
season one, episode one,

00:04:56.690 --> 00:04:59.130
showed members of the Night's
Watch going beyond the Wall

00:04:59.130 --> 00:05:01.562
and coming face-to-face
with a White Walker.

00:05:01.562 --> 00:05:03.645
The side narrative throughout the show

00:05:03.645 --> 00:05:06.490
was that the wars between
wannabe kings of Westeros

00:05:06.490 --> 00:05:08.540
were actually nothing
compared to the threat

00:05:08.540 --> 00:05:10.453
that was building beyond the Wall.

00:05:11.500 --> 00:05:13.470
When we were introduced to the Night King,

00:05:13.470 --> 00:05:16.470
he immediately became
the ultimate villain,

00:05:16.470 --> 00:05:20.210
incredibly powerful and
seemingly undefeatable.

00:05:20.210 --> 00:05:23.860
So when this enemy was defeated
midway through season eight,

00:05:23.860 --> 00:05:26.210
it seemed like a bit of an anticlimax,

00:05:26.210 --> 00:05:28.390
not to mention the way
in which it happened.

00:05:28.390 --> 00:05:30.860
First of all, we didn't get
the see any of the skilled

00:05:30.860 --> 00:05:34.486
swordsmen battling the
Night King nor his henchmen.

00:05:34.486 --> 00:05:36.860
I just want to make it
clear, I have no problem

00:05:36.860 --> 00:05:39.270
with Arya being the person
who killed the Night King.

00:05:39.270 --> 00:05:40.660
I actually really liked this,

00:05:40.660 --> 00:05:43.980
but we were teased this main event

00:05:43.980 --> 00:05:47.150
of Jon and the Night King having a battle.

00:05:47.150 --> 00:05:49.070
He didn't get to fight him at Hardhome.

00:05:49.070 --> 00:05:50.810
He didn't get to fight him on the lake,

00:05:50.810 --> 00:05:53.060
and it just felt like
the Battle of Winterfell

00:05:53.060 --> 00:05:54.930
was where it was going to happen,

00:05:54.930 --> 00:05:57.420
yet what we got was Jon chasing him around

00:05:57.420 --> 00:06:00.058
and being blocked by an undead dragon,

00:06:00.058 --> 00:06:02.855
and the Night King not fighting anyone

00:06:02.855 --> 00:06:06.791
and then being killed
without a battle by Arya.

00:06:06.791 --> 00:06:10.320
It just felt a little bit
like an anticlimax to me.

00:06:10.320 --> 00:06:11.970
Another anticlimax for myself

00:06:11.970 --> 00:06:14.790
was the death of Cersei and Jaime.

00:06:14.790 --> 00:06:16.470
The story arc of the sibling lovers

00:06:16.470 --> 00:06:19.960
threatened more than once to
end in betrayal or murder,

00:06:19.960 --> 00:06:22.740
but in the end, the two
were killed by falling rocks

00:06:22.740 --> 00:06:24.690
while trying to run away from Daenerys

00:06:24.690 --> 00:06:27.600
and Drogon's fire frenzy on the Red Keep.

00:06:27.600 --> 00:06:28.950
A little bit of a letdown.

00:06:29.830 --> 00:06:32.860
Another anticlimax was Jon ending up

00:06:32.860 --> 00:06:34.150
back at the Night's Watch.

00:06:34.150 --> 00:06:36.280
I know this is a circular story

00:06:36.280 --> 00:06:39.130
and George R.R. Martin does like those,

00:06:39.130 --> 00:06:43.182
but it didn't feel like it was
what his character deserved.

00:06:43.182 --> 00:06:46.860
Jon had an epic storyline with
his true heritage revealed

00:06:46.860 --> 00:06:49.450
as Aegon Targaryen, but
it all came to nothing,

00:06:49.450 --> 00:06:51.510
and he found himself back at Castle Black

00:06:51.510 --> 00:06:53.350
going ranging with the wildlings.

00:06:53.350 --> 00:06:55.540
Despite Arya killing the Night King,

00:06:55.540 --> 00:06:58.103
I do feel like she was
underused in this series.

00:06:58.103 --> 00:07:00.580
Since killing the Freys, we never saw her

00:07:00.580 --> 00:07:02.660
use her faceless training again,

00:07:02.660 --> 00:07:04.700
which could have been for
some more interesting viewing

00:07:04.700 --> 00:07:07.460
than seeing her ride off
on a random white horse.

00:07:07.460 --> 00:07:09.730
It was pretty anticlimactic for myself

00:07:09.730 --> 00:07:12.950
that the Unsullied and
the Dothraki just left.

00:07:12.950 --> 00:07:16.190
The final episode shows the
Unsullied and Dothraki armies

00:07:16.190 --> 00:07:18.320
hyped for more wars at the start

00:07:18.320 --> 00:07:20.340
but ends with them leaving in boats,

00:07:20.340 --> 00:07:22.600
watching the person who killed their queen

00:07:22.600 --> 00:07:25.220
walk past without doing anything.

00:07:25.220 --> 00:07:27.830
They say time's a healer, but I'm not sure

00:07:27.830 --> 00:07:29.370
it works wonders like that.

00:07:29.370 --> 00:07:31.770
Four, plot holes.

00:07:31.770 --> 00:07:34.310
Season eight was "Game of
Thrones"' final season,

00:07:34.310 --> 00:07:36.540
so all of the loose ends
should have been tied up,

00:07:36.540 --> 00:07:40.380
and the audience shouldn't be
left asking questions, right?

00:07:40.380 --> 00:07:41.297
Wrong.

00:07:41.297 --> 00:07:43.856
Here are just a few that
are keeping me up at night.

00:07:43.856 --> 00:07:46.590
So what about Bran becoming the king?

00:07:46.590 --> 00:07:49.000
As much as I personally like this twist,

00:07:49.000 --> 00:07:50.808
it was a little unbelievable

00:07:50.808 --> 00:07:52.024
due to the way that it happened,

00:07:52.024 --> 00:07:54.420
especially when all the people deciding

00:07:54.420 --> 00:07:57.439
seem to have a far more obvious choice.

00:07:57.439 --> 00:07:59.530
Tyrion loves stories, right?

00:07:59.530 --> 00:08:02.400
And who had a better
story than Bran Stark?

00:08:02.400 --> 00:08:03.730
How about Jon?

00:08:03.730 --> 00:08:06.580
Jon went from Stark
bastard to Lord Commander

00:08:06.580 --> 00:08:08.130
to King in the North.

00:08:08.130 --> 00:08:10.610
He was literally killed and resurrected.

00:08:10.610 --> 00:08:12.140
He was the Prince that was Promised

00:08:12.140 --> 00:08:14.719
who slayed his lover for
the good of the realm.

00:08:14.719 --> 00:08:16.970
Now, let's send that
guy to the Night's Watch

00:08:16.970 --> 00:08:20.313
where he can watch over what, exactly?

00:08:21.150 --> 00:08:23.480
No one even talked about
him as a possibility

00:08:23.480 --> 00:08:26.200
when they were choosing the
next leader of Westeros.

00:08:26.200 --> 00:08:28.890
The reason for this: It
was part of an agreement

00:08:28.890 --> 00:08:31.550
to please the Unsullied and Yara Greyjoy,

00:08:31.550 --> 00:08:34.800
who felt that Jon should be
punished for killing Daenerys.

00:08:34.800 --> 00:08:37.330
While this is understandable
to a certain degree,

00:08:37.330 --> 00:08:39.550
it feels hollow as the Unsullied are seen

00:08:39.550 --> 00:08:42.340
minutes later boarding a
ship to the Isle of Naath,

00:08:42.340 --> 00:08:45.320
and the Greyjoys were not a
powerful-enough house to demand

00:08:45.320 --> 00:08:48.202
this sort of respect from
the other lords of Westeros.

00:08:48.202 --> 00:08:50.890
Sansa and Arya both had great stories

00:08:50.890 --> 00:08:53.380
worthy of becoming queen, but again,

00:08:53.380 --> 00:08:55.555
neither of them were mentioned.

00:08:55.555 --> 00:08:59.040
So Bran became king, but
how can the Three-Eyed Raven

00:08:59.040 --> 00:09:00.799
actually become king anyway?

00:09:00.799 --> 00:09:03.650
Bran went from this
strange, emotionless person

00:09:03.650 --> 00:09:05.710
who would stare blankly
before saying something

00:09:05.710 --> 00:09:09.530
that made others feel uncomfortable
to the king of Westeros,

00:09:09.530 --> 00:09:12.000
and none of the characters
even batted an eyelid.

00:09:12.000 --> 00:09:14.350
The last Three-Eyed Raven
lived north of the Wall

00:09:14.350 --> 00:09:16.450
and merged with a tree.

00:09:16.450 --> 00:09:18.060
Why is Bran so different?

00:09:18.060 --> 00:09:20.410
That's just another thing
that isn't explained.

00:09:21.260 --> 00:09:24.150
Who is the Prince of Dorne,
how did he come to power,

00:09:24.150 --> 00:09:27.260
and why are the Martells
suddenly so unimportant

00:09:27.260 --> 00:09:30.020
in season eight despite
being such a big focus

00:09:30.020 --> 00:09:31.410
of earlier seasons?

00:09:31.410 --> 00:09:35.680
And finally, why does the
Night's Watch even exist anymore?

00:09:35.680 --> 00:09:39.100
The White Walkers are wiped
out, wildlings are allies,

00:09:39.100 --> 00:09:40.720
the Wall is destroyed near East Watch,

00:09:40.720 --> 00:09:43.290
and various castles along
the Wall are unmanned.

00:09:43.290 --> 00:09:45.130
Is there any point in restoring them?

00:09:45.130 --> 00:09:48.020
Five, forgotten characters.

00:09:48.020 --> 00:09:50.230
It became very clear in the final seasons

00:09:50.230 --> 00:09:53.450
that a few characters
really served no purpose

00:09:53.450 --> 00:09:55.430
to the final outcome of the show.

00:09:55.430 --> 00:09:56.850
Where's Jaqen H'ghar?

00:09:56.850 --> 00:09:58.840
The Faceless Man is one of the characters

00:09:58.840 --> 00:10:00.330
who trained Arya Stark.

00:10:00.330 --> 00:10:03.270
At the end of season six, he
let her go back to Westeros,

00:10:03.270 --> 00:10:05.397
and we haven't heard from him since.

00:10:05.397 --> 00:10:07.638
What about Daario Naharis?

00:10:07.638 --> 00:10:10.530
The former lover of
Daenerys was left in Meereen

00:10:10.530 --> 00:10:13.413
at the end of season six
to keep an eye on the city.

00:10:13.413 --> 00:10:15.460
We never heard from him again.

00:10:15.460 --> 00:10:16.960
Surely his men could have been used

00:10:16.960 --> 00:10:20.275
in the battle against
the dead, apparently not.

00:10:20.275 --> 00:10:22.186
And what about Ellaria Sand?

00:10:22.186 --> 00:10:24.850
At the end of season seven,
Ellaria Sand was left

00:10:24.850 --> 00:10:27.580
to watch her daughter die in
front of her in the Red Keep.

00:10:27.580 --> 00:10:30.200
We never saw or heard from her again.

00:10:30.200 --> 00:10:33.132
Six, unrealistic events.

00:10:33.132 --> 00:10:35.360
Yes, I am going to go there.

00:10:35.360 --> 00:10:38.240
In a show about dragons,
magic, and the undead,

00:10:38.240 --> 00:10:41.021
I'm gonna call out some
unrealistic things that happened.

00:10:41.021 --> 00:10:43.916
Don't worry, the irony isn't lost on me.

00:10:43.916 --> 00:10:46.610
First of all, let's
talk about how far Arya

00:10:46.610 --> 00:10:48.908
must have had to jump
to kill the Night King.

00:10:48.908 --> 00:10:51.350
Yep, that looks like a long way.

00:10:51.350 --> 00:10:54.106
I know she has skills,
but that seems silly.

00:10:54.106 --> 00:10:57.040
How about when Dany
didn't see Euron's ships

00:10:57.040 --> 00:10:58.393
from half a mile high?

00:10:59.500 --> 00:11:03.260
This unlikely ambush resulted
in Rhaegal being killed

00:11:03.260 --> 00:11:06.500
and Dany's chances of taking
King's Landing being reduced.

00:11:06.500 --> 00:11:08.650
One of the positives about having a dragon

00:11:08.650 --> 00:11:11.950
should be that you can see
things coming, apparently not.

00:11:11.950 --> 00:11:15.520
During this ambush, Drogon and
Dany fled from Euron's ships,

00:11:15.520 --> 00:11:17.180
but when it came to taking King's Landing,

00:11:17.180 --> 00:11:19.510
the pair destroyed his
whole fleet with ease,

00:11:19.510 --> 00:11:22.740
along with all the scorpions
on the wall at King's Landing.

00:11:22.740 --> 00:11:24.847
Why didn't they just do that before?

00:11:24.847 --> 00:11:27.050
Remember when Bronn
aggressively threatened

00:11:27.050 --> 00:11:29.000
Tyrion and Jaime with a crossbow?

00:11:29.000 --> 00:11:30.990
Well, apparently that was
forgiven pretty easily

00:11:30.990 --> 00:11:33.360
following the burning the King's Landing.

00:11:33.360 --> 00:11:35.740
What about undead Viserion?

00:11:35.740 --> 00:11:38.360
So undead Viserion
burned through the Wall,

00:11:38.360 --> 00:11:40.940
which had protected the
Seven Kingdoms for years,

00:11:40.940 --> 00:11:43.470
but he couldn't burn
through a mound of stones

00:11:43.470 --> 00:11:44.513
to kill Jon Snow.

00:11:45.890 --> 00:11:48.840
So No. 7, the changing
of the main villain.

00:11:48.840 --> 00:11:51.580
From episode three to six,
we saw three different

00:11:51.580 --> 00:11:54.693
main villains: the Night King
at the Battle of Winterfell,

00:11:54.693 --> 00:11:57.430
Cersei during episode four and five,

00:11:57.430 --> 00:12:00.290
and finally Daenerys in episode six.

00:12:00.290 --> 00:12:02.480
This quick switching
between the antagonists

00:12:02.480 --> 00:12:04.870
was a little confusing,
and it wasn't helped

00:12:04.870 --> 00:12:08.030
by the lack of time for all
of these events to sink in.

00:12:08.030 --> 00:12:10.203
Eight, character deaths.

00:12:11.150 --> 00:12:13.520
All right, so I think this
show would have actually been

00:12:13.520 --> 00:12:15.906
better with more of the
main characters dying.

00:12:15.906 --> 00:12:18.770
"Game of Thrones" lost a lot
of of its unpredictability

00:12:18.770 --> 00:12:20.320
when it was drawing to a close.

00:12:21.340 --> 00:12:23.380
The show has done it over and over again

00:12:23.380 --> 00:12:26.610
killing main characters,
yet it seemed pretty obvious

00:12:26.610 --> 00:12:28.500
that the key characters
were going to survive

00:12:28.500 --> 00:12:30.580
the Battle of Winterfell as it was clear

00:12:30.580 --> 00:12:33.190
that their storylines
still needed to play out.

00:12:33.190 --> 00:12:36.610
I never felt like there was
a danger that Jon, Dany,

00:12:36.610 --> 00:12:40.002
Tyrion, Sansa, or Arya
might die in the battle,

00:12:40.002 --> 00:12:42.270
which is something that
just wouldn't have happened

00:12:42.270 --> 00:12:43.880
in previous seasons.

00:12:43.880 --> 00:12:46.860
Nine, continuity mistakes.

00:12:46.860 --> 00:12:49.740
Yes, this season had quite a few.

00:12:49.740 --> 00:12:51.830
The most memorable was the coffee cup

00:12:51.830 --> 00:12:55.570
in plain sight on the table
after the Battle of Winterfell.

00:12:55.570 --> 00:12:57.820
There are also two
modern-day water bottles

00:12:57.820 --> 00:13:00.420
on the floor in the scene
before Bran is named king.

00:13:01.400 --> 00:13:03.977
Something that many fans
noticed is the inconsistency

00:13:03.977 --> 00:13:07.010
of the way that King's
Landing was depicted.

00:13:07.010 --> 00:13:10.330
All of these are apparently the same city,

00:13:10.330 --> 00:13:12.080
yet they don't look anything alike.

00:13:13.652 --> 00:13:15.560
And finally, eagle-eyed viewers spotted

00:13:15.560 --> 00:13:17.260
that Daenerys is wearing different wigs

00:13:17.260 --> 00:13:20.593
when she arrived at Winterfell
in season eight, episode one.

00:13:21.470 --> 00:13:23.700
Let me know in the comments
if you think I missed anything

00:13:23.700 --> 00:13:25.413
or if you disagree altogether.

